our screenshot shows:

Plain Text
Unable to connect to the server:
dial tcp 192.168.49.2:8443: connect: no route to host

This usually means Minikube is stopped or the Kubernetes API server is not running.

1. Check Minikube Status
Shell
minikube status

If you see:

Plain Text
Stopped

start it:

Shell
minikube start
2. Verify Node

After Minikube starts:

Shell
kubectl get nodes
 

Expected:

Plain Text
NAME STATUS ROLES AGE
minikube Ready control-plane
3. Check Current Context
Shell
kubectl config current-context

Expected:

Plain Text
minikube
 

If not:

Shell
kubectl config use-context minikube
4. Test Cluster Connectivity
Shell
kubectl cluster-info

Expected:

Plain Text
Kubernetes control plane is running at https://192.168.49.2:8443
5. Check ArgoCD Pods

Once Minikube is running:

Shell
kubectl get pods -n argocd

You should see:

Plain Text
argocd-server
argocd-repo-server
argocd-application-controller
argocd-dex-server
argocd-redis

with status:

Plain Text
Running
6. Open ArgoCD UI Again

Run:

Shell
kubectl port-forward svc/argocd-server -n argocd 8080:443

Then open:

Plain Text
https://localhost:8080
If minikube status Fails

Run:

Shell
minikube delete
minikube start --driver=docker

Then:

Shell
kubectl get nodes
kubectl get pods -n argocd

Please run:

Shell
minikube status

and share the output. That will confirm whether the cluster is stopped or if the kubeconfig needs fixing.




